From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jim Martin <jimmartin@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 20:27:07 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 2851/2856] thunderbolt: qcom: Add Glymur USB4 host router 1 (v2 MCU
 layout)

The second USB-C port of the Surface Laptop 8 is served by host router
1, which the driver rejected. Router 1 has the v2 microcontroller
layout: the control registers at 0x14000 instead of 0x22000, the RAM at
0x1e000 instead of 0x13000, the DROM 0x2000 higher, and its own
firmware image. The MCU helper already knows the offsets.

Describe the routers in a table (layout, start-up profile, RAM size,
lane status register) and use it where router 0's values were built in:
the MCU interrupt registers, the event ring bounds and the lane status
polled after sleep.

Add the router 1 start-up profile. It is the cold start of router 0
with the differences of the v2 layout:

- CBCR_FORCE_MEM_CORE_ON on the microcontroller clock after the
  firmware upload;
- adapter bits 21, 20 and 19 instead of 23, 22 and 21 on a cold start;
  the warm restart clears 23, 22 and 21 on both layouts;
- a set-up routine of 17 register updates before the MCU starts, cold
  and warm;
- the router index in the DROM;
- on MCU event 6, bits 7:6 of the two lane adapter registers cleared.

The set-up routine reaches registers that need a second power domain,
the one of the PCIe controller the router tunnels. On the Surface
Laptop 8 a read of router register 0xe030 with that domain off resets
the SoC; with it on the whole routine runs. Attach every power domain
of a router that has more than one, link them to its runtime PM, keep
them on the wakeup path with the router, and refuse to probe router 1
without the second one. The platform's ACPI tables list that domain
for router 0 too; router 0's start-up does not touch those registers
and its node is left as it is.

Router 1 gets no DisplayPort owner here: the DP IN handling is tied to
router 0 and DisplayPort controller 0, so a dock on router 1 tunnels
USB3 but no display.

The sequence follows the platform's Windows driver. On the Surface
Laptop 8 a Thunderbolt 4 dock on the second port comes up at two lanes
of 20 Gb/s with its USB3 tunnel, in both plug orientations, after an
unplug and replug, after system sleep and at boot.

Seen once each in those runs and not explained: the dock's USB2 hub
failed to enumerate after a flipped plug-in (one of about eight
plug-ins), and the dock's Ethernet adapter received nothing after one
of two sleeps until it was re-attached.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5.2
Signed-off-by: Jim Martin <jimmartin@gmail.com>
